# TLS is not optional for this application, and the reason is stronger than the cookie.
#
# The camera page calls getUserMedia(), and browsers only expose that in a secure context.
# Over plain http to anything other than localhost, navigator.mediaDevices is not merely
# restricted -- it is undefined, and the page cannot open a camera at all. So a kiosk that
# is not the server itself needs https to *function*, quite apart from SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE
# wanting it to be safe.
#
# Two situations, pick one.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. LAN only, no public DNS name. This is the usual case for a camera by a door.
#
# `tls internal` makes Caddy run its own certificate authority and issue itself a
# certificate. A public CA cannot help here: they will not issue for a private address.
#
# One manual step, and it is not optional. Install Caddy's root certificate on the kiosk
# machine so the browser trusts it:
#
#     docker compose cp caddy:/data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt .
#
# then add that file to the machine's trust store. Do NOT rely on clicking through the
# browser's warning instead -- an overridden certificate error leaves the page in a state
# where camera access is unreliable, which is the one thing this deployment needs.
#
# LIST EVERY NAME AND ADDRESS THE KIOSK WILL TYPE, and understand why the list cannot be
# left empty. A bare `:443` looks like the obvious way to say "serve on 443 whatever the
# host is called", and it is a trap: the site block then has no subject name, `tls
# internal` has nothing to issue a certificate FOR, and Caddy starts cleanly, logs
# nothing, writes no certificate, and rejects every handshake with an internal error.
# The failure is silent on the server and total on the client.
#
# Names not in this list are not served. Add the server's LAN address here -- an IP is
# fine, Caddy issues internal certificates with IP SANs -- alongside the two that make
# the stack testable on the machine that runs it:
#
#     kiosk.lan, 192.168.1.50, localhost, 127.0.0.1 {
#
localhost, 127.0.0.1 {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy app:8000
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. A real hostname that resolves publicly, with ports 80 and 443 reachable.
#
# Delete the block above, uncomment this, and put your hostname in. Caddy obtains a
# Let's Encrypt certificate on first start and renews it forever with no cron job, no
# certbot, and no reload hook to discover is broken in ninety days.
#
# attendance.example.org {
# 	reverse_proxy app:8000
# }
